course: dictionary
lesson: 7732
title: A word that is not English
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Sometimes the word you see is not an English word at all, or it is a typing slip. Pul is one of these. Read the clues around it before you decide what it means: is it about money in another country, a word from another language, or a mistake for another word?
explain: Here is a sentence with pul in it: «un pull ("PUL") is a jumper, borrowed from the English word pullover and made shorter.» The words "is a jumper" tell you the meaning. The sentence also shows that a word can travel from one language to another and change a little on the way.
explain: If pul does not fit a sentence, ask whether pull was meant. Swap pull in and see if the sentence now makes sense. This simple check is often the best dictionary you have.
fact: Pul is not a normal English word.
fact: A pul is a small coin of Afghanistan, and 100 puls make one afghani.
fact: Pul is also the Persian and Dari word for «money», so you may find it in texts from those languages.
fact: In English texts, pul is often just a misspelling of «pull», so check whether that was meant.
fact: Today the pul is hardly used.
exercise: In the sentence «un pull ("PUL") is a jumper», what does pul mean? | A spelling of the word pool | *A jumper | Money in English | A small coin of Afghanistan
exercise: A text says: «Please pul the door shut.» What was probably meant? | pal | pill | pool | *pull
exercise: What is a pul? | A type of football | *A small coin of Afghanistan | A large note from England | A sweet from the shop
test: How many puls make one afghani? | 10 | 1000 | 50 | *100
test: What should you do if pul does not fit in an English text? | *Check whether pull was meant, then swap it in and see if it makes sense | Guess a random word that starts with P | Shout it out loud | Give up and skip the whole text
exam: Which is the best first step when you meet pul in an English text? | Change it to pool without checking | Skip every sentence with a P in it | *Check the words around it, and ask whether pull was meant | Look up the nearest coin in a museum
